WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.567
SG: Hello, we are here today with Dr. Wally Gilbert, who won the Nobel Prize

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.533
in Chemistry in 1980 for discovering one of the first methods

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:26.300
for sequencing DNA. Dr. Gilbert is an incredibly accomplished scientist.

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.200
He actually started out as a physicist

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.133
and became interested in molecular biology

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.600
after he was faculty at Harvard and met Jim Watson

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.633
and started working with him on mRNA.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:41.633
Since then he has made key discoveries in how translation works.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:45.133
He biochemically isolated the first genetic control element.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:49.900
He proposed... he was the first to propose the existence of introns and exons.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:52.467
He was the first to propose the RNA world hypothesis.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.733
He's made significant advances in biotechnology

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.833
and recombinant DNA and in cloning.

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:03.033
And he co-founded Myriad Genetics and Biogen.

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:09.567
And since about the early 2000s, he's actually started another field,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.600
and that is that of photography,

00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:13.833
where he's become an incredibly accomplished photographer.

00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:16.333
And he's had over 40 solo exhibits.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:19.600
And we are actually here today in his studio in Somerville, Massachusetts.

00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:21.667
So thank you so much for joining us today.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.100
WG: Thank you,  Sarah.

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:27.100
SG: I thought we'd just start by... I'd start by asking you how you got interested in photography.

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.200
WG: In some ways I've been interested in photography all of my life.

00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:35.567
I used a camera as a child, and I had a wet darkroom

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.567
and did that sort of photography

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.233
where I would use a camera, record objects in the world,

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:48.633
photograph a bit as I traveled. I didn't do anything in particular with those images.

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:59.000
And I traveled at one time around 2000 in Italy, and the photographs

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:02.800
I took, from that trip and a trip in Egypt before that

00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:05.800
struck me as very, very lovely,

00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:10.500
and I began to explore whether I could make large images from those.

00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:14.900
And in those days it was a 2 mega pixel camera.

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:17.367
People insisted that you could not make a large image

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.633
They were suitable only for postcard size images.

00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:24.367
And I was assured by everybody that I couldn't make large images.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.533
But I simply tried to do it and discovered that one, it was very easy to do,

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:34.067
and the large images had an emotional impact that small ones didn't.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.200
And so I began to do photography seriously as an art form.

00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:42.467
At first I would show just to friends

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:48.767
and people began to buy some of the images.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:55.100
And a professor, Jan Kubasiewicz at Massachusetts College of Arts

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:59.100
saw some of the images and gave me an art show in 2004.

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:03.433
And we did a very large show down at the Mass. College of Art

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:09.867
and that included 8 foot by 12 foot images.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.633
And I've been doing the art more and more seriously ever since.

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:20.167
We did a very large installation in Poland.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:27.667
Again we did it through Jan Kubasiewicz and a friend of his, Zuk.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:35.000
A friend of his in Poland who had access to a site in the middle of Warsaw

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:42.233
that was an ancient factory and now was used, and then, somewhat as an art display space.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:44.767
And they invited me to come and photograph in the factory,

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.900
thinking we would do some sort of installation.

00:03:46.933 --> 00:03:50.400
And I went and photographed and then we created an installation

00:03:50.433 --> 00:03:53.167
with 8 foot by 12 foot hangings

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.133
of photographs of fragments of machines hung throughout the factory.

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:03.867
And about 30 two foot by three foot images,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.100
and that display was up in Warsaw for 2 months,

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:12.300
and then again in Lodz and in Posnan. It's been put on again in several places.

00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:17.467
SG: And then you moved into more abstract art?

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.533
WG: Then I moved from these fragments of machines

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:23.033
to a set of photographs of ballet dancers.

00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:30.467
I was invited to illustrate a book that a friend, Christine Temin,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.033
one of the writers of the Boston Ballet

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:35.400
so I photographed ballet dancers rehearsal for several years.

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:40.000
And those photographs are quite beautiful.

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:45.033
They are very lovely people and lovely bodies and lovely emotions.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:50.100
But that led me from photographing the people to making abstract images

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:52.733
from the dancers,

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:54.733
and that led me to into a series of abstract works,

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:58.467
of which one of them is here behind you.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:06.067
These abstract... I spent a year sort of working with these abstract forms,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.233
first with these abstract forms based on a human face,

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:15.333
and then moved into more and more strange color spaces and pattern spaces.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:20.000
And then I spent another year or so working purely in abstract forms

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:22.800
based on geometric figures,

00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:26.933
squares and triangles and lines.

00:05:26.967 --> 00:05:30.867
And we have actually a show up right now in Germany, in Lindau.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.967
You can see them there of those abstract, geometric, abstract figures.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.333
And then in the more recent years, I have gone back to pure photography

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:42.733
and I do photographs of black and white,

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:47.533
and very brilliant color, and I have no idea what I will do next.

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:51.533
SG: What made you decide to explore different mediums?

00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:55.133
WG: Umm, well it's again sort of constant curiosity.

00:05:55.167 --> 00:06:01.300
The... Again one of the similarities between the curiosity that drives

00:06:01.333 --> 00:06:03.833
one as a scientist, which is a curiosity about the world,

00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:11.133
and if you are driven by that curiosity, you change mediums that you work with.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:16.200
You change what you are doing. You change the technologies you use.

00:06:16.233 --> 00:06:20.733
And so I find it perfectly natural to explore different mediums.

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:27.167
SG: So, many people think of art and science as being two completely separate things,

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.333
what do you think about it? WG: I think they both

00:06:31.367 --> 00:06:34.333
have great similarities between the art and the science.

00:06:34.367 --> 00:06:38.933
I find that the creative drive is very, very similar.

00:06:38.967 --> 00:06:45.333
And the fascination with what is new. Both in science and in art,

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:48.433
the drive is a creative drive to create something new.

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:51.733
In science, you try to create something that is new

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:54.167
and a new fact about the world.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.000
Something that is new and different.

00:06:57.033 --> 00:06:59.167
And true and beautiful.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.600
With art you try to create something that is new,

00:07:02.633 --> 00:07:08.133
beautiful and true. The stress in the art is on the beauty.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:11.500
The question of truth is a much more minor one.

00:07:11.533 --> 00:07:13.867
And the stress in science is on truth

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:17.233
in the sense of a socially defined sort of truth,

00:07:17.267 --> 00:07:21.767
in which the rest of the world has to agree that what we found

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:27.700
or the theoretical idea or the experiment can be built upon or can be reproduced.

00:07:27.733 --> 00:07:32.633
The art is a lot more individual than the science.

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:38.967
And the rewards are in the effect the art has on the viewer.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.200
The rewards in science are finding something that is new,

00:07:42.233 --> 00:07:46.500
and again, passing that knowledge on to the world.

00:07:46.533 --> 00:07:50.200
I am laughing because I was also thinking of another view

00:07:50.233 --> 00:07:57.800
of science. Al Hershey was a great scientist and used to talk about Hershey heaven.

00:07:57.833 --> 00:07:59.933
Hershey heaven was having an experiment that worked

00:07:59.967 --> 00:08:01.567
and doing it over and over and over again.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.900
And some people do art that way.

00:08:03.933 --> 00:08:06.633
They find an art form that works and they do that over and over and over again.

00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:09.399
I have a slightly different cast of mind.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.700
I can't bear to do things over and over again.

00:08:11.733 --> 00:08:14.800
I constantly go on and do something different,

00:08:14.833 --> 00:08:18.133
and that was true both in the science and in the art.

00:08:18.167 --> 00:08:24.733
I never continued doing the same thing in science for very long.

00:08:24.767 --> 00:08:28.733
And that had problems, I should say.

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:32.767
That has grant problems. It also has other problems,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:35.200
but I'd be constantly trying to do something different in science

00:08:35.233 --> 00:08:37.800
and I find myself doing something different in the art.

00:08:37.833 --> 00:08:43.800
SG: So has your time as a scientist influenced the way you approach doing art?

00:08:43.833 --> 00:08:46.133
WG: I was originally a theoretical physicist.

00:08:46.167 --> 00:08:52.267
And the theoretician tries to understand the world within a grain of sand.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.233
Something to be sketched on the back of an envelope.

00:08:55.267 --> 00:08:59.867
I then became an experimental biologist.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.300
An experimenter has a somewhat different view of the world.

00:09:02.333 --> 00:09:05.833
They are trying to find the world through struggle with it,

00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:10.733
through experimentation, rather than through what would necessarily be broad concepts.

00:09:10.767 --> 00:09:14.367
The art that I do I think of as an experimental art,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.933
rather than a conceptual one.

00:09:16.967 --> 00:09:23.133
I am not trying to see if a broad idea that I could have somebody else do

00:09:23.167 --> 00:09:25.800
because it can be outlined for them.

00:09:25.833 --> 00:09:30.833
I find the photography sort of constantly to be doing.

00:09:30.867 --> 00:09:37.667
I find the art that I do in the computer almost a constant experiment,

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:38.733
almost like experimental science.

00:09:38.767 --> 00:09:43.533
I treat the computer program in the computer as an experimental instrument.

00:09:43.567 --> 00:09:50.133
I build the art up piece by piece

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:57.433
as an experimentalist, creating forms in the computer either based on a photograph,

00:09:57.467 --> 00:10:02.800
or based on a drawing, and continuing until I find something that I like.

00:10:02.833 --> 00:10:08.433
When I find something I like, I keep a copy of it and go on and see how else I can modify it.

00:10:08.467 --> 00:10:10.867
It's a very experimental approach.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:14.000
SG: You've been very successful in many different areas.

00:10:14.033 --> 00:10:17.267
First as a theoretical physicist, and then as a molecular biologist,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.233
and biochemist, and then you went into biotechnology,

00:10:21.267 --> 00:10:25.000
and now you are a successful artist.

00:10:25.033 --> 00:10:27.467
Do you have any keys to success, or advice that you would give

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:32.333
to people who are interested in transitioning?

00:10:32.367 --> 00:10:39.167
I do think that people do not change what they do enough.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.767
It is often far easier to change what you are doing than you imagine.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.967
Very often people think they have been trained to do a certain thing.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.967
When you run across students, you feel you are being trained to do something

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.900
and there should be a job just in that thing I am being trained for waiting for me.

00:10:55.933 --> 00:11:02.733
And that is the future of my life. It is very important to realize that the real role in our education

00:11:02.767 --> 00:11:08.067
is to teach us how to think broadly, not just about particular topics.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:18.533
The PhD degree is an exercise in how to think, how to reason for yourself what is true.

00:11:18.567 --> 00:11:22.333
And I learned that when I changed from physics to biology.

00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:28.367
I got a PhD in Physics, theoretical physics, and now suddenly I found myself doing experiments in biology.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:34.400
I realized that the underlying ability that I had learned doing the PhD

00:11:34.433 --> 00:11:37.933
how to think about, how to convince myself that something is true,

00:11:37.967 --> 00:11:39.367
or how to test some ideas for myself,

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:42.067
was the crucial element.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:46.000
And the element in doing experimental biology

00:11:46.033 --> 00:11:49.300
was an experimental technique that I could learn very easily

00:11:49.333 --> 00:11:52.633
and facts that I could ask somebody else about.

00:11:52.667 --> 00:11:55.067
But the real question is how do you put something together,

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.733
was something I learned much more broadly as part of the PhD.

00:11:59.767 --> 00:12:03.800
I don't think people realize that enough.

00:12:03.833 --> 00:12:05.767
People spend five years getting a PhD

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:07.933
and think that must mean that it takes five years

00:12:07.967 --> 00:12:10.167
to learn how to do anything different.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:14.400
But in fact, the five years, six years in this country,

00:12:14.433 --> 00:12:20.233
is mostly spent in gradually learning how to reason for oneself

00:12:20.267 --> 00:12:21.933
and decide for oneself what is true.

00:12:21.967 --> 00:12:25.567
And that is not something we teach very effectively.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.667
We teach it by example. We teach it by a sort of guild system

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.133
putting people to work gradually, questioning them gradually,

00:12:33.167 --> 00:12:35.967
and letting them struggle and find the answers.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.833
The actual material that is involved is something one can master within a year or a few months,

00:12:42.867 --> 00:12:48.167
but the ability to work with it is what takes the time to develop.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.200
And once that ability is developed it can be moved to other fields.

00:12:51.233 --> 00:12:56.900
So it is much easier to change fields than people imagine.

00:12:56.933 --> 00:13:00.333
SG: Well, thank you very much for speaking with us today.

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:06.333
And I guess we'll just end by could you tell people where they can find your work online.

00:13:06.367 --> 00:13:07.700
WG: If you look me up online as Wally Gilbert,

00:13:07.733 --> 00:13:14.733
you will find my websites listed under photography.

00:13:14.767 --> 00:13:17.867
I am not the silversmith, and I am not the baseball player,

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.400
but otherwise you'll find me that way.

